Translation patch: <RistrettoPoint as MultiscalarMul>::multiscalar_mul

Change: the lazy `points.map(|P| P.borrow().0)` becomes a `Vec` built by a `for` loop.
Why: Aeneas only models `Iterator::next` (aeneas#1057).
Behaviour: same result for valid inputs. Alone, not identical: the callee's `size_hint` asserts see an
exact `Vec` (so e.g. `filter` inputs no longer panic, infinite points no longer terminate).
With edwards-multiscalar_mul.patch applied, the behaviour is as declared there.

diff --git a/curve25519-dalek/src/ristretto.rs b/curve25519-dalek/src/ristretto.rs
index 269662d..a3f6f18 100644
--- a/curve25519-dalek/src/ristretto.rs
+++ b/curve25519-dalek/src/ristretto.rs
@@ -968,13 +968,16 @@ impl MultiscalarMul for RistrettoPoint {
     fn multiscalar_mul<I, J>(scalars: I, points: J) -> RistrettoPoint
     where
         I: IntoIterator,
         I::Item: Borrow<Scalar>,
         J: IntoIterator,
         J::Item: Borrow<RistrettoPoint>,
     {
-        let extended_points = points.into_iter().map(|P| P.borrow().0);
+        let mut extended_points = vec![];
+        for P in points {
+            extended_points.push(P.borrow().0);
+        }
         RistrettoPoint(EdwardsPoint::multiscalar_mul(scalars, extended_points))
     }
 }
 
 #[cfg(feature = "alloc")]
